# 🏢 Enterprise LAN — Configuration Guide

## 📋 Network Plan — VLAN Table

| VLAN ID | Name | Subnet | Gateway |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Default | 192.168.1.0/24 | 192.168.1.254 |
| 10 | HR | 192.168.10.0/24 | 192.168.10.254 |
| 20 | IT | 192.168.20.0/24 | 192.168.20.254 |
| 99 | Servers | 192.168.99.0/24 | 192.168.99.254 |
| 100 | Admin | 192.168.100.0/24 | 192.168.100.254 |

> **Inter-VLAN routing** is handled by the router using subinterfaces (Router-on-a-Stick).

---

## ⚙️ Step 01 — Switch Configuration (SW1 & SW2)

### 1.1 — Create VLANs

```bash
vlan 10
 name HR
vlan 20
 name IT
vlan 99
 name Servers
vlan 100
 name Admin
```

### 1.2 — Access Ports (client-facing)

```bash
interface gi0/X
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan X
```

### 1.3 — Trunk Ports (switch-to-switch / switch-to-router)

```bash
interface gi0/X
 switchport trunk encapsulation dot1q
 switchport mode trunk
```

---

## ⚙️ Step 02 — DHCP Server Configuration

> Give the DHCP server a **static IP** in its VLAN (VLAN 99) + its gateway.
> File: `/etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf`

```bash
# VLAN 1 — Default
subnet 192.168.1.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {
  range 192.168.1.100 192.168.1.200;
  option routers 192.168.1.254;
}

# VLAN 10 — HR
subnet 192.168.10.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {
  range 192.168.10.100 192.168.10.200;   # ⚠️ fix: was 192.168.1.200 (typo)
  option routers 192.168.10.254;
}

# VLAN 20 — IT
subnet 192.168.20.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {
  range 192.168.20.100 192.168.20.200;
  option routers 192.168.20.254;
}

# VLAN 99 — Servers
subnet 192.168.99.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {   # ⚠️ fix: was /32 (typo)
  range 192.168.99.100 192.168.99.200;
  option routers 192.168.99.254;
}

# VLAN 100 — Admin
subnet 192.168.100.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {
  range 192.168.100.100 192.168.100.200;
  option routers 192.168.100.254;
}
```

> Then start the DHCP service:
```bash
systemctl start isc-dhcp-server
systemctl enable isc-dhcp-server
```

---

## ⚙️ Step 03 — Router Configuration (Router-on-a-Stick)

> The port between the **router and switch must be a trunk** to carry all VLANs.
> Each VLAN gets a **subinterface** with its IP + `ip helper-address` pointing to the DHCP server.

```bash
# VLAN 1
interface gi0/0.1
 encapsulation dot1q 1
 ip address 192.168.1.254 255.255.255.0
 ip helper-address 192.168.99.10

# VLAN 10 — HR
interface gi0/0.10
 encapsulation dot1q 10
 ip address 192.168.10.254 255.255.255.0
 ip helper-address 192.168.99.10

# VLAN 20 — IT
interface gi0/0.20
 encapsulation dot1q 20
 ip address 192.168.20.254 255.255.255.0
 ip helper-address 192.168.99.10

# VLAN 99 — Servers
interface gi0/0.99
 encapsulation dot1q 99
 ip address 192.168.99.254 255.255.255.0
 ip helper-address 192.168.99.10

# VLAN 100 — Admin
interface gi0/0.100
 encapsulation dot1q 100
 ip address 192.168.100.254 255.255.255.0
 ip helper-address 192.168.99.10
```

